---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import { site } from "../../data/site";
import { blogPostingSchema } from "../../lib/schema";

const slug = "hurricane-watch-vs-warning";
const title = "Hurricane Watch vs Warning: What's the Difference and When to Act";
const description =
  "A hurricane watch means dangerous conditions are possible within 48 hours, while a warning means they are expected. Learn the difference and when Florida and Texas households should act.";
const canonical = `${site.url}/blog/${slug}/`;
const date = "2026-08-07";
const jsonLd = blogPostingSchema({
  title,
  description,
  url: canonical,
  datePublished: date,
  dateModified: date,
  authorName: "HurricaneHub team"
});
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} canonical={canonical} jsonLd={jsonLd} lastModified={date} ogImage="https://www.hurricanetracker.cc/images/blog/og/hurricane-watch-vs-warning.png">
  <main class="prose">
    <article>
      <h1>{title}</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        When a storm approaches the Gulf or the Atlantic, the single most misunderstood pair of terms is
        <strong>hurricane watch</strong> and <strong>hurricane warning</strong>. They sound similar, but the
        difference is the difference between "get ready" and "take action now." Confusing them is one of the
        most common reasons households lose precious hours.
      </p>

      <h2>What a Hurricane Watch Means</h2>
      <p>
        A <strong>hurricane watch</strong> is issued by the National Hurricane Center (NHC) when hurricane
        conditions (sustained winds of 74 mph or higher) are <em>possible</em> within a defined coastal area,
        typically within 48 hours. "Possible" is the key word. The storm may still weaken, shift, or miss your
        county entirely. A watch is a heads-up: conditions could develop, so prepare while you still have time
        and daylight.
      </p>
      <p>
        If you live along a vulnerable coastline — for example, where the
        <a href="/hurricane-tracker/florida/">Florida hurricane tracker</a> already shows a system organizing in
        the Caribbean — a watch is your signal to finish shutters, fuel the car, and confirm your evacuation
        zone. You are not yet told to leave, but you should be ready to leave on short notice.
      </p>

      <h2>What a Hurricane Warning Means</h2>
      <p>
        A <strong>hurricane warning</strong> is more serious. It is issued when hurricane conditions are
        <em>expected</em> within the area, usually within 36 hours. "Expected" means the forecast is confident
        enough that you should complete preparations immediately and follow any evacuation orders without
        waiting. Warnings are not guesses — they reflect a high-confidence track and intensity forecast.
      </p>
      <p>
        On the <a href="/hurricane-tracker/texas/">Texas hurricane alerts</a> page, a warning often arrives as a
        storm strengthens in the Gulf and the cone narrows toward the coast. Once a warning is posted for your
        zone, the window for safe travel shrinks fast. Roads clog, fuel runs out, and bridge closures begin. The
        warning is the moment to execute the plan you built during the watch.
      </p>

      <h2>Why the Distinction Matters for Your Family</h2>
      <p>
        The watch-to-warning transition is when most families make or break their outcome. A watch is the calm
        before the storm — use it. A warning is the storm before the storm — act on it. Treating a warning like
        a watch ("I'll wait and see") is how people get caught in rising water or trapped by closed roads.
      </p>
      <p>
        Remember that a hurricane warning covers wind, but the deadliest hazards are often water: storm surge,
        rainfall flooding, and isolated tornadoes. A warning for your county may be issued for surge even when
        you expect "only" tropical-storm winds.
      </p>

      <h2>When to Act: A Simple Rule</h2>
      <p>
        Use this checklist the moment a watch is issued for your area, and finish it before a warning arrives:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Charge phones and battery packs; download offline maps and the HurricaneHub dashboard.</li>
        <li>Refill prescriptions and gather a 7-day supply of medication and pet food.</li>
        <li>Photograph documents, IDs, and property for insurance; store copies in cloud storage.</li>
        <li>Confirm your evacuation zone and identify two routes inland.</li>
        <li>Secure outdoor items and complete window protection before winds rise.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        When the warning is issued, stop preparing and start executing: leave if ordered, or shelter in your
        hardened safe room if you are outside the evacuation zone.
      </p>

      <p class="byline">
        This article was updated for the 2026 hurricane season by the HurricaneHub team.
      </p>
    </article>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .prose {
    max-width: 720px;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 2rem 1.25rem 3rem;
    line-height: 1.7;
  }
  .prose h1 {
    font-size: 2rem;
    line-height: 1.2;
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
  }
  .prose h2 {
    font-size: 1.35rem;
    margin: 2rem 0 0.75rem;
  }
  .lead {
    font-size: 1.1rem;
    color: var(--muted, #4a5568);
  }
  .prose a {
    color: var(--accent, #1f6feb);
    text-decoration: underline;
  }
  .byline {
    margin-top: 2.5rem;
    padding-top: 1rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--border, #e2e8f0);
    font-style: italic;
    color: var(--muted, #4a5568);
  }
</style>
